  "heading": "Three balance readings define an LOD result",
  "text": "An LOD figure comes from three balance readings: the tare (the empty, previously dried weighing bottle alone), the pre-drying total (bottle plus sample before drying starts) and the post-drying total (bottle plus residue when drying stops). WHO's gravimetric procedure places the sample, accurately weighed, in a previously dried and tared flat weighing bottle, and drying ends when two consecutive weighings differ by no more than 5 mg, unless the test procedure specifies otherwise.[1] Calculate: LOD% = (pre-drying total − post-drying total) ÷ (pre-drying total − tare) × 100%. The denominator is the net sample mass — the basis WHO states as “Calculate the loss of weight in mg per g of air-dried material.”[1] The ×100 only writes that same ratio as a percentage. The sample quantity itself follows the applicable test procedure.[1]"
},

  },
  {
    "heading": "Do not turn a qualified exception into a blanket rule",
    "text": "Q3C allows a nonspecific method such as loss on drying when only Class 3 solvents are present and the method is properly validated.[1] That qualified statement is not a license to identify individual solvents from an unexplained LOD value. Equally, it would be wrong to claim Q3C always requires headspace GC for every extract. Request the laboratory’s documented rationale for the chosen procedure and the actual acceptance question."
  },
  {
    "heading": "Release checklist and unresolved items",
    "text": "Before disposition, reconcile the SKU and lot, complete process declaration, justified solvent list, applicable acceptance basis, method suitability, results and reporting capability. Record who reviews exceptions and what additional evidence is needed. No universal residual-solvent limit is proposed here, and no customer dose, test result, solvent-free claim or ZL batch compliance is invented. A supplier statement and a batch-specific measurement remain different kinds of evidence."
  }
